<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salamis, Cyprus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Constantia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salamis was an ancient city on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, north of modern Famagusta, founded in the 11th century BCE as successor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enkomi">Enkomi</a>. Its kings were often the strongest on the island, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evagoras-i">Evagoras I</a> in the 4th century BCE. As Constantia it was the metropolitan see of Cyprus in Late Antiquity, and it was abandoned after Arab raids in the 7th century; extensive ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus.jpg" alt="Salamis, Cyprus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerhard Haubold · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gymnasion_und_Therme_aus_S%C3%BCdwest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Salamis lay on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> at the mouth of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedieos">Pedieos</a>, 6 km (3.7 mi) north of modern Famagusta. According to tradition its founder was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teucer">Teucer</a>, son of Telamon, king of the Greek island of Salamis, who could not return home after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> because he had failed to avenge his brother Ajax. There is little direct evidence for this myth, and some evidence that the area was occupied long before, with the town developing as a replacement when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enkomi">Enkomi</a> was cut off from the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Children's burials in Canaanite jars indicate a Phoenician presence, and Assyrian inscriptions name the town among the kingdoms of Iadnana, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> was called. In 708 BCE the city-kings of Cyprus paid homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, but they kept relative independence as long as they paid tribute, which let them gather wealth and power. The royal tombs of Salamis hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and rich grave goods, much of it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and burial customs such as the sacrifice of horses echo Homeric rites. The first coins were minted in the 6th century BCE.</p><p>In 450 BCE Salamis was the site of a simultaneous land and sea battle between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> and the Persian Empire. It was later besieged and conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a>. Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evagoras-i">Evagoras I</a> (411–374 BCE) Greek culture and art flourished. In 306 BCE Demetrius I of Macedon defeated the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> off Salamis and captured the island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 58 BCE, and the governor's seat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nea-paphos">Nea Paphos</a>, though Salamis kept its wealth and the Emperors Trajan and Hadrian restored its public buildings.</p><p>Paul the Apostle and Barnabas landed at Salamis on their first journey, and tradition holds that Barnabas was stoned to death there around 61 CE. Earthquakes destroyed the town early in the 4th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> rebuilt it as Constantia, and it became an episcopal seat, its best-known occupant being Saint Epiphanius. The silting of the harbour led to a gradual decline, and the city was finally abandoned during the Arab invasions of the 7th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins are very extensive, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, amphitheatre, stadium and public baths; the theatre could seat up to 15,000. The necropolis covers around 7 km2 to the west of the town. Excavations began in the late nineteenth century under the Cyprus Exploration Fund, and finds from them are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salamis, Cyprus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the following century Cypriot towns declined or moved, Aegean settlers arrived, and the island reorganised into the Iron Age kingdoms such as <b>Salamis</b>, Paphos and Kition.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-alashiya">Kingdom of Alashiya</a></li><li><blockquote>Salamis remained a major city, renamed <b>Constantia</b>, until abandoned after the Arab raids of the 7th century CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-salamis">Kingdom of Salamis</a></li><li><blockquote>Its king Aristocyprus fell fighting the Persians at <b>Salamis</b> in 497 BCE, after which Soloi endured a five-month siege.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-soloi">Kingdom of Soloi</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Great later gave it to the king of <b>Salamis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tamassos">Kingdom of Tamassos</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome their chief town moved from Crociatonum to Cosedia, renamed <b>Constantia</b> in late antiquity, the origin of Coutances and of the name Cotentin.</blockquote><a href="/state/unelli">Unelli</a></li></ul></section>
